<header class="wrap hero">
  <span class="kicker">set up in about 2 minutes</span>
  <h1>How Pounce connects<br>to <em>your computer</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">Pounce doesn't run your agents in the cloud — it's a remote control for the coding agents <b>already running on your own machine</b>. Install the app on your computer, scan one QR with your phone, and you're paired.</p>
  <p class="fine">No account · no Pounce server in the middle · your code and conversations stay on your devices.</p>

  <div class="needs">
    <div class="need"><span class="ic">🖥️</span><div><h4>A computer</h4><p>Your Mac, Windows, or Linux machine running coding agents like Claude, Codex, Cursor, or opencode.</p></div></div>
    <div class="need"><span class="ic">📱</span><div><h4>The Pounce app</h4><p>On your iPhone (live on the App Store) — Android is in testing on Google Play. It's the remote: it shows every agent and lets you steer them.</p></div></div>
    <div class="need"><span class="ic">📶</span><div><h4>A network path</h4><p>Same Wi-Fi just works. Away from home, Pounce's own secure tunnel connects you — same QR, nothing to set up.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section style="padding-top:24px">
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- STEP 1 -->
    <div class="step-row reveal">
      <div class="step-copy">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <h3>Install it on your computer</h3>
        <p>On a <b>Mac</b>, download the <b>Pounce desktop app</b>. On <b>Windows or Linux</b>, run the headless <b>Bridge</b>. Either one hosts the coding agents on that machine automatically — no terminal, no config files.</p>
        <div class="tip"><span>⌨️</span><span>Terminal person? <b class="mono">npx use-pounce</b> does the same in one line — it even works over <b>SSH</b>, so you can pair a server you never sit in front of.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-visual">
        <figure class="mac"><img src="/assets/desktop-app.png" alt="The Pounce desktop app on macOS — hosting the coding agents on this machine, ready to pair" /></figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 2 -->
    <div class="step-row flip reveal">
      <div class="step-copy">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <h3>A pairing code appears</h3>
        <p>The app shows a QR code and the address it's listening on. This is the handshake — it carries the machine's address and a private, per-machine token so only your phone can connect.</p>
        <div class="tip"><span>🔒</span><span>The token stays on your devices. Pairing once is enough — Pounce remembers the machine, and reaches it over its own secure tunnel when you're away.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-visual">
        <div class="mac">
          <div class="mac-bar"><span class="tl r"></span><span class="tl y"></span><span class="tl g"></span><span class="mac-t">Pounce</span></div>
          <div class="bridge">
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px"><span class="paw">🐾</span><span class="bt">Pounce</span></div>
            <p class="sub">Scan with your phone to connect</p>
            <div class="qrwrap"><img src="/assets/pair-qr.svg" alt="Pounce pairing QR code" /></div>
            <div class="addr">pounce · Dirghas-Mac-mini</div>
            <div class="status"><span class="sdot"></span><span>Waiting for your phone…</span></div>
            <p class="hint">Open Pounce on your phone → Sync a device → Scan this code.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 3 -->
    <div class="step-row reveal">
      <div class="step-copy">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <h3>Scan it with Pounce</h3>
        <p>Open Pounce on your phone, tap <b>Sync a device</b>, and point the camera at the code. That's the entire setup — no typing addresses, no copying tokens.</p>
        <div class="tip"><span>📷</span><span>You can also scan with the built-in Camera app — it opens Pounce straight to the pairing screen.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-visual phone-stage">
        <div class="phone-glow"></div>
        <div class="phone">
          <div class="screen">
            <div class="scan">
